    {
      "id": "f16-l3",
      "title": "Computed GO TO",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "GO TO (10, 20, 30), K jumps to the first label if K is 1, the second if K is 2, and so on. If K is outside the list, FORTRAN just goes on with the next card.",
        "It was FORTRAN's version of a menu or a case statement: read a code from a card, and jump to the right piece of the program.",
        "Programs full of GO TOs became so hard to follow that Edsger Dijkstra wrote his famous letter \"Go To Statement Considered Harmful\" in 1968. Pascal and C were built to need them less."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "      DO 50 K = 1, 4",
          "      GO TO (10, 20, 30), K",
          "      WRITE (6,1) K",
          "      GO TO 50",
          "   10 WRITE (6,2)",
          "      GO TO 50",
          "   20 WRITE (6,3)",
          "      GO TO 50",
          "   30 WRITE (6,4)",
          "   50 CONTINUE",
          "    1 FORMAT (' NO LABEL FOR', I2)",
          "    2 FORMAT (' ADD')",
          "    3 FORMAT (' SUBTRACT')",
          "    4 FORMAT (' PRINT')",
          "      STOP",
          "      END"
        ],
        "output": [
          "ADD",
          "SUBTRACT",
          "PRINT",
          "NO LABEL FOR 4"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "f16-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "K is 2. Where does GO TO (10, 20, 30), K jump?",
          "options": [
